[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce an ongoing fault. End-to-end health looks good now:
- Sustained pings between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 showed 0% loss over 40 packets each way with stable 0.7–1.8 ms RTT. Path MTU is correct (DF pings at 1472 bytes succeed; 1473 fails as expected for 1500-byte MTU). 
- iperf from pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 averaged ~127–131 Mbit/s with only 3 retransmits over 5 seconds.
- Interfaces on hosts and routers are UP with zero errors/drops; tc qdisc shows no drops.
- BGP across the fabric is Established everywhere with no drops; routing tables are consistent.

What likely caused the “cutting in and out” earlier is a recent control-plane restart and BGP reconvergence. Evidence: all BGP peerings showed short uptimes (~2 minutes) when checked, and spine_router_0_0’s FRR log records a restart at 13:14:46 with transient BGP connection errors immediately before sessions re-established. During those few moments, the leafs temporarily lacked the remote /24 route, which would interrupt active flows (what was reported as “sessions dropping”).

Localization: fabric control-plane (FRR/bgpd) on multiple routers; not a single interface. Root cause: FRR daemons were recently restarted, causing transient route churn and brief loss of reachability during BGP reconvergence.

Corrective fix: No action required now; the fabric is stable. To minimize future impact of control-plane events, consider enabling BFD for eBGP and configuring BGP maximum-paths on the leafs to use both spines, and optionally lowering BGP keepalive/hold timers to speed convergence.
=== END ===